---
import Docs from '../../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import Callout from 'web-design-system/astro/marketing/Callout.astro'
import CodeBlock from 'web-design-system/astro/product/CodeBlock.astro'
import TableScroll from '../../../components/atoms/TableScroll.astro'

const toc = [
  { label: 'Requirements', href: '#requirements' },
  { label: 'Run from a checkout', href: '#checkout' },
  { label: 'Test mode', href: '#test-mode' },
  { label: 'Build a desktop bundle', href: '#bundle' },
  { label: 'Install an agent', href: '#agents' },
  { label: 'First project', href: '#first-project' },
]

const requirements = [
  { need: 'macOS', detail: 'macOS 13 (Ventura) or newer.' },
  { need: 'Linux', detail: 'Ubuntu 24.04+ with WebKitGTK 2.44 or newer.' },
  { need: 'Git', detail: 'Agent tasks need a Git repository; Shepherd creates their worktrees.' },
  { need: 'Nix + direnv', detail: 'The repository dev shell supplies the pinned Rust, Node, pnpm and helper scripts.' },
  { need: 'An agent CLI', detail: 'Install and sign in to the agent you want to use. Shepherd does not bundle one.' },
]

const run = `direnv allow .   # once, from the repository root
pnpm install
shep`

const testRun = `shep --test
# or, in a non-interactive shell:
direnv exec . shep --test`

const build = `direnv exec . pnpm --dir apps/shepherd build
# macOS bundle:
# target/release/bundle/macos/Shepherd.app
# Ubuntu package:
# target/release/bundle/deb/*.deb`
---

<Docs
  title="Install and run"
  description="Run Shepherd from a checkout today; signed desktop installers are still being prepared."
  toc={toc}
>
  <Callout tone="warning" title="No signed installer yet">
    Shepherd currently runs from a checkout. Signed macOS and Linux artifacts are on the
    <a href="/docs/users/releases">release roadmap</a>; do not treat a development bundle as a
    released installer.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
  <TableScroll label="Shepherd requirements">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>You need</th><th>Details</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          requirements.map((entry) => (
            <tr><td>{entry.need}</td><td>{entry.detail}</td></tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TableScroll>
  <p>
    The development shell is the supported path because it brings the exact toolchain and helper
    scripts. If you already have a working Rust and pnpm setup, the checkout commands below are the
    short version.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkout">Run from a checkout</h2>
  <CodeBlock filename="terminal" code={run} showLineNumbers={false} />
  <p>
    <code>shep</code> starts the desktop app and its Vite server. Development data lives in
    <code>.cache/test-db/shepherd.db</code>, so projects and chats survive a normal restart. Use
    <code>direnv exec . shep</code> when you need to launch it from a non-interactive runner.
  </p>

  <h2 id="test-mode">Test mode</h2>
  <p>
    <code>shep --test</code> starts on its own port with an empty database in a fresh directory
    under <code>.cache/test-db/</code>. Changes survive Tauri reloads during that launch; closing the
    app discards the session database, and the next test launch starts empty again.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock filename="terminal" code={testRun} showLineNumbers={false} />
  <p>
    Set <code>SHEPHERD_DEV_DATA_DIR</code> to an absolute path to keep a separate project list and
    database. <code>SHEPHERD_DEV_PROJECT_ROOT</code> points the debug project picker at a fixture
    directory.
  </p>

  <h2 id="bundle">Build a desktop bundle</h2>
  <p>
    The app build sets the macOS deployment target to 13.0. Use the bundle command rather than
    copying a debug binary around:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock filename="terminal" code={build} showLineNumbers={false} />
  <p>
    <code>cargo test -p shepherd --test packaging</code> checks that the Tauri config, build script
    and package workflow still agree. The clean-machine release gate is documented in the
    <a href="/docs/contributors/release-pipeline">release pipeline</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="agents">Install an agent</h2>
  <p>
    Shepherd starts installed agent CLIs as subprocesses. It probes the executable, chooses the
    richest protocol the installed version supports, and shows the tier and fallback reason in the
    task and diagnostics. Install and authenticate the agent first; Shepherd never stores provider
    keys.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Codex</strong> — install the CLI and sign in. The desktop app can supply its local project list for batch import, but the CLI must still be reachable.</li>
    <li><strong>OpenCode</strong> — install the executable, or point <code>agents.opencode.executable</code> at an existing one in <code>settings.json</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Claude Code</strong> — configure an ACP executable with <code>agents.claude.executable</code> and optional <code>agents.claude.args</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Custom ACP agent</strong> — add its executable and arguments in settings if it speaks ACP over stdio.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The <a href="/docs/users/configuration">configuration page</a> has the settings paths and examples.
    If an agent is missing, the chat shows the install hint and offers a terminal as the working
    alternative.
  </p>

  <h2 id="first-project">First project</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Add a local directory. Agent tasks require a Git repository; the folder picker accepts either a <code>.git</code> directory or a <code>.git</code> file pointing at a worktree.</li>
    <li>Select the project and type a prompt. Sending the first prompt creates the task, its branch and its worktree.</li>
    <li>Answer any approval, then open the Review, Git or Files tab to inspect what changed.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Continue with the <a href="/docs/users/mental-model">mental model</a> before running several
    tasks in parallel; it explains what each task owns and what survives a restart.
  </p>
</Docs>
